# RP2040 in BOOTSEL mode presents as USB mass storage labelled RPI-RP2. A
# headless printer has no desktop automounter, so nothing mounts it and
# mcu-updater's bootsel flasher has no volume to copy the .uf2 onto.
#
# Mounted under its own USB topology path rather than one fixed directory:
# a single mountpoint means two boards in BOOTSEL collide, and one bystander
# board blocks flashing the one you meant. ID_PATH_TAG (not ID_PATH) is
# systemd's sanitized form, safe inside a directory name; the path_id builtin
# in 60-persistent-storage.rules sets it, which runs before this 99- rule.
# systemd-mount will create the mountpoint's parent directories itself, but
# a tmpfiles.d entry pre-creates them anyway so they are owned by %USER% at a
# known mode rather than whatever systemd-mount's own defaults would leave.
# See docs/bootsel-mountpoint-design.md.
#
# Two rules, not one guarded by ID_PATH_TAG=="?*": if ID_PATH_TAG is ever
# unset (a hub path_id cannot name, or a device caught by an earlier
# TEST=="whole_disk" rule), mounting at ".../by-path/" with an empty leaf
# would mount onto the by-path directory itself - shadowing every other
# board's mountpoint and the legacy fallback both. The second rule below
# catches that case and gives it its own fixed leaf instead of a silent
# no-op or a shadow-mount.
#
# On unplug, `--collect` garbage-collects the transient mount *unit* but leaves
# the mountpoint directory behind, so an empty directory per port accumulates.
# There is deliberately no ACTION=="remove" rule here to clean that up: version
# 3 had one, and hardware showed it loses a race it cannot win. udev runs the
# remove event while systemd is still tearing the mount down, so the rmdir hit
# a still-mounted directory and failed EBUSY on every unplug, logging an error
# each time and removing nothing. The cleanup lives in the tmpfiles.d entry
# instead, where the daily sweep runs long after any unmount has settled.
# `bootsel_scan` ignores stale directories regardless - it gates on
# INFO_UF2.TXT, never on the directory name - so this is tidiness, not
# correctness.
#
# Installed by mcu-updater's install.sh. %USER% is substituted there.
# The version marker below is what install.sh compares to decide whether an
# already-installed rule needs replacing - bump it whenever this file changes.
# mcu-updater-bootsel-rule-version: 4
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="block", ENV{DEVTYPE}=="partition", ENV{ID_FS_LABEL}=="RPI-RP2", ENV{ID_PATH_TAG}=="?*", \
  RUN{program}+="/usr/bin/systemd-mount --no-block --collect --owner=%USER% $devnode /media/%USER%/BOOTSEL/by-path/$env{ID_PATH_TAG}"
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="block", ENV{DEVTYPE}=="partition", ENV{ID_FS_LABEL}=="RPI-RP2", ENV{ID_PATH_TAG}!="?*", \
  RUN{program}+="/usr/bin/systemd-mount --no-block --collect --owner=%USER% $devnode /media/%USER%/BOOTSEL/by-path/unknown"
